# 0009. One app module per device type; same-APK surfaces stay in `:app`

Date: ${SCAFFOLD_DATE}
Status: Accepted

## Context

The app may grow beyond phones, tablets and foldables: Wear OS, Android TV, Android Automotive OS, Android
Auto, XR headsets and glasses, Googlebook/desktop, Cast, widgets. Each has its own UI toolkit, manifest
requirements, quality guidelines and (for some) its own Play track.

## Decision

Following the modularization guide ("if your app targets multiple device types, define an app module for
each one"):
- **Own app module, same applicationId, own Play track**: `:wear` (Wear Compose Material 3), `:tv` (Compose
  for TV), `:automotive` (AAOS, `CarAppActivity` / parked activity).
- **Inside `:app`** (same APK): adaptive phone/tablet/foldable/desktop layouts, XR headset differentiation
  (`uses-feature android.software.xr.api.spatial required=false`), Android Auto (via the `:car` library),
  XR glasses (projected activity, `:glasses` library), Cast sender, Glance widgets (`:widget` library).
- Form-factor apps share `:core:model`/`:core:domain`/`:core:data`, never the phone UI modules.

## Consequences

- Easier: each device's dependencies stay out of the others' APKs; each module follows its platform's
  quality tier; shared business logic is written once.
- Harder: more app modules to sign and release (the release workflow builds them all); a feature may need a
  form-factor-specific UI module per device.
